{"status":"source_error","reasoning":"The earlier answer 3\sqrt{3}/2, option b, agrees with Math Academy's stated answer. The recorded submission was instead 1+\sqrt{2}, option a, so its Incorrect grade is consistent. Lagrange equations give equal cosines; cosine is injective on (0,\pi), so all angles equal \pi/3. Concavity of sine proves the global maximum is 3\sin(\pi/3)=3\sqrt{3}/2. The source's concluding note incorrectly treats comparison with one other triangle as sufficient proof of a maximum. This proof error does not change the answer.","confidence":1,"answers":[{"key":"selection","correct_value":"\frac{3\sqrt{3}}{2}","correct_option":"b","value_type":"math","wrong_value":"1+\sqrt{2}","correct_keys":[],"wrong_keys":[],"ma_value":"\frac{3\sqrt{3}}{2}","ma_basis":"worked_solution","ma_evidence":"Therefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(\frac{\pi }{3},\frac{\pi }{3},\frac{\pi }{3}),$ and the maximum value is 

$$
\begin{aligned}f(\frac{\pi }{3},\frac{\pi }{3},\frac{\pi }{3}) & =\operatorname{sin}⁡(\frac{\pi }{3})+\operatorname{sin}⁡(\frac{\pi }{3})+\operatorname{sin}⁡(\frac{\pi }{3}) \\  & =\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}+\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}+\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \\  & =\frac{3\sqrt{3}}{2}.\end{aligned}
$$","disagrees":false}]}
